The documented differences between the SX1262 command stream of LilyGO's
RadioLib build, driven as LilyGO's phone app drives it (golden_lora.cc), and
ours (coproc/src/lora_k230.c). coproc/test/board_trace/run.sh applies this
to the vendor's trace with `patch --fuzz=0` and requires the result to be our
trace, byte for byte. Trace lines: C <bytes> (one CS-framed command, MOSI),
RESET (a pulse on NRESET). Hunk by hunk:

Hunks 1 and 2, startReceive: LilyGO's app (Lora.cpp 286, 294-298) asks for a
  10 s receive (SetRx 0x09C400 x 15.625 us) every 100 ms, maps RX_DONE,
  TIMEOUT and HEADER_VALID to DIO1 (0x0212) and polls the IRQ register; ours
  receives continuously (SetRx 0xFFFFFF, RadioLib's RX_TIMEOUT_INF) with the
  receive IRQs RADIOLIB_SX126X_IRQ_RX_DEFAULT (RX_DONE, TIMEOUT, CRC_ERR,
  HEADER_ERR = 0x0262) on DIO1 too, and reads DIO1 (IO20) before any SPI
  traffic. The other commands of startReceive are the same.

Hunk 3, before startTransmit: SetStandby, as RadioLib's own transmit()
  does (SX126x.cpp 224-226); LilyGO's app calls startTransmit straight from
  receiving.

Hunk 4, after TX_DONE: ours clears the IRQs and goes to standby
  (finishTransmit, SX126x.cpp 573-579) and receives again (the startReceive
  of hunks 1-2); LilyGO's app only looks at the IRQ register (Lora.cpp
  128-130) and re-arms receiving from its timer.

Not visible in this trace: ours reads a packet from the start pointer
  GetRxBufferStatus reports (byte 1 of `13 00 00 00`), as LilyGO's Linux
  RadioLib does (LT-BSP RadioLib SX126x.cpp 556-565); LilyGO's RT-Smart
  RadioLib reads from offset 0. The model reports offset 0, so both send
  `1E 00 00 ...`.

